-- title:
--   Kronecker's theorem, general form — the closure of $\{t\tau \bmod \mathbb Z^N : t\in\mathbb R\}$ is the annihilator of the integer relations of $\tau$
-- statement:
--   **The orbit closure of a linear flow on the torus (Kronecker's theorem, general form).** Let $N\ge 0$, $\mathbb T=\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$, and for $\tau=(\tau_1,\dots,\tau_N)\in\mathbb R^N$ let $\overline\gamma_\tau\colon\mathbb R\to\mathbb T^N$, $\overline\gamma_\tau(t)=(t\tau_1,\dots,t\tau_N)\bmod\mathbb Z^N$, be the one-parameter subgroup generated by $\tau$. Let
--   $$\Lambda_\tau=\{a\in\mathbb Z^N:\ a_1\tau_1+\dots+a_N\tau_N=0\}$$
--   be the group of integer linear relations among the coordinates of $\tau$. Then the closure of the orbit is the subgroup of $\mathbb T^N$ cut out by these relations:
--   $$\overline{\overline\gamma_\tau(\mathbb R)}=\bigl\{\xi\in\mathbb T^N:\ a_1\xi_1+\dots+a_N\xi_N=0\ \text{in}\ \mathbb T\ \text{for every}\ a\in\Lambda_\tau\bigr\}.$$
--
--   Identifying the character group of $\mathbb T^N$ with $\mathbb Z^N$ via $\chi_a(\xi)=a_1\xi_1+\dots+a_N\xi_N$, this says that the annihilator $G^\perp$ of the closed subgroup $G=\overline{\overline\gamma_\tau(\mathbb R)}$ is $\Lambda_\tau$ and that $G=(G^\perp)^\perp$. It is the explicit form of Theorem B.5.2.1(1) of the source obtained in its proof (pp. 628–629: for $\chi=\chi_a$, "the defining condition of $G^\perp$ [...] reads $\sum_k\tau_ka_k=0$"), combined with Pontryagin duality for closed subgroups of a compact abelian group. It contains Kronecker's density theorem ($\Lambda_\tau=0$ iff the orbit is dense) and determines the subtorus on which the flow equidistributes in (B.5.2.1).
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The index set $\{1,\dots,N\}$ is an arbitrary finite type `ι` (so $N=0$ is allowed). $\mathbb T$ is Mathlib's `UnitAddCircle = AddCircle (1 : ℝ)`, the coercion `ℝ → UnitAddCircle` is the quotient map $\mathbb R\to\mathbb R/\mathbb Z$, and $\mathbb T^N$ is `ι → UnitAddCircle` (Mathlib's `UnitAddTorus ι`) with the product topology; $\overline\gamma_\tau(t)$ is written inline as `fun k => ((t * τ k : ℝ) : UnitAddCircle)`. The relation $a\in\Lambda_\tau$ is `∑ k, (a k : ℝ) * τ k = 0` and the condition on $\xi$ is `∑ k, a k • ξ k = 0`, with the $\mathbb Z$-module structure of `UnitAddCircle`.
-- source:
--   A. M. Masullo, The Music of the Primes, draft of 20 November 2026, https://math216.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/the-music-of-the-primes.pdf, pp. 627–629, Theorem B.5.2.1(1) and its proof (determination of the annihilator $G^\perp$ of the orbit closure); classical reference: B. Hasselblatt and A. Katok, A First Course in Dynamics. With a Panorama of Recent Developments, Cambridge University Press, 2003, Part 1, Theorem 4.1.15 and §4.1.6 (of which the source's theorem is the multi-dimensional version); P. Humphreys, The Summatory Function of Liouville's Function and Pólya's Conjecture, BPh thesis, Australian National University, 2010, §4.3, proof of Lemma 4.3 (general case, as cited by the source)

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace KroneckerWeyl

theorem closure_range_torusFlow {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] (τ : ι → ℝ) :
    closure (Set.range fun t : ℝ => fun k : ι => ((t * τ k : ℝ) : UnitAddCircle)) =
      {ξ : UnitAddTorus ι | ∀ a : ι → ℤ, ∑ k, (a k : ℝ) * τ k = 0 → ∑ k, a k • ξ k = 0} := by
  sorry

end KroneckerWeyl
